# The curated command index behind the welcome screen and `cmds`.
#
# Tab-separated, five fields: COMMAND, GROUP, COLUMN, BLURB, DESCRIPTION.
#
#   GROUP        which icon and colour `cmds` shows the row with. One of the
#                keys in pick-command's table: system, data, files, shell,
#                code, secrets, agents, ops, shortcuts. The section comments
#                below mirror it for readability, but the field is what counts.
#   COLUMN       0 = `cmds` only. 1-3 = also printed on the welcome screen, in
#                that column, in the order the lines appear below.
#   BLURB        the welcome screen cell text. Empty when COLUMN is 0.
#   DESCRIPTION  what `cmds` searches, and its preview fallback.
#
# One file so the screen cannot drift from the picker: the screen is a view of
# this list, not a second copy of it. The screen's geometry is a hard budget —
# 7 lines by 78 columns, three cells of 26 (a 9-wide name, a space, a 16-wide
# blurb) — so a fourth entry in any column, a name past 9 characters or a blurb
# past 16 silently overflows it. tests/commands-index_spec.sh pins all of that,
# and that every command here still resolves.
#
# What belongs here: anything you would type at a prompt and might forget the
# name of. Not servers, LSPs or GUI apps; not libraries or the plumbing behind
# preview/yazi/nvim; not names you could never forget (`curl`, `make`, `grep`).
#
# Descriptions are the one line tldr cannot give you — why this is on the
# machine and when to reach for it. `cmds` previews the tldr page or `--help`
# output for the how-to, and falls back to this text when there is neither.
#
# Rows for a tool that only some machines install are gated on the SAME
# condition its package manifest uses, so this source stays the union of every
# profile and each machine renders only what it actually has. Copy the
# condition from the manifest rather than inventing one — if they disagree, the
# row is offered on a box where the command does not exist.
#
# The welcome-screen rows (columns 1-3) are deliberately never gated: that
# keeps the 7-line by 78-column budget identical on every profile, so the
# geometry test on one machine speaks for all of them.

# ─── the welcome screen: column 1, system & network ───────────────────────────
procs	system	1	'ps'	Process list as a readable table, with tree view and colour
duf	system	1	disk usage	Disk usage and free space per mount, as a table
bandwhich	system	1	net usage	Live per-process network bandwidth (needs sudo on macOS)
doggo	system	1	DNS lookup	DNS lookup with readable output — a modern dig
gping	system	1	ping graph	Ping as a live graph, several hosts at once
hyperfine	system	1	benchmark	Benchmark a command with warmups, statistics and comparison

# ─── the welcome screen: column 2, data & text ────────────────────────────────
